/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md:3:**Snapshot.** Read-only download from the Kaggle episode API began 2026-09-25 and captured every completed episode returned then: **121 `es1_g1`** (last captured end 01:29 UTC) and **66 `es1_g9`** (last captured end 01:31 UTC). The ladder remains live, so this is a reproducible snapshot, not a claim about later matches. Fingerprints are in `research/census_ours/fp/`; detailed re-simulated reports for all losses below −$1k are in `runs/t024_replay_reports.txt`.
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md:5:**Method.** `census.py mine --workers 4` re-simulated all 187 files with kaggle-environments 1.32.7. `replay_report.py` was then run on every 27 loss below −$1k. A real g1 win, episode 112889566, encodes a timeout as `"action": null`; `replay_mine` now normalizes that to `{}` (commit `d6f3ba4`) and this replay re-simulates to its recorded $144,828/$19,832 outcome.
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md:7:Kaggle replay metadata supplies team names but no opponent submission id, source or rank. Therefore “Horizon descendant” cannot be identified honestly from these files. The only reproducible family proxy below is **same terminal macro**: exact equality of final `animals` and bought `seeds` dictionaries. It is descriptive, not ancestry.
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md:98:- **Koba vs g9**, episodes 113043248 (−$5,390) and 113065629 (−$16,943): Koba takes SW on day 8 in both, versus our day 11. In the latter match, Koba sells $66.6k strawberry and $38.6k milk; ours is $43.6k and $29.5k. Our lead at day 15 (+$5.3k) becomes −$10.0k by day 18 and −$16.8k by day 21. In 113043248 we sell 3,635 wheat for $143k but spend $158k total, while Koba sells only 471 wheat, spends $20.8k and still finishes ahead. This is direct evidence against blindly adding more wheat throughput.
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md:99:- **Matt Motoki vs g1**, episodes 113084650/113088169/113090464 (−$6,267/−$3,788/−$14,051): Matt takes SW on day 9 each time, versus our day 11. Their late mix uses fewer melons (54 vs our 72) and much less fertilizer selling (185–205 vs our 328–341), with more carrot and tomato revenue. In 113090464 our day-18 third-land purchase and extra hiring raise total cost to $36.0k vs $25.6k while their tomato revenue is $35.8k vs $18.9k; our deficit grows from −$1.4k d18 to −$15.8k d27.
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md:101:- **MKGA vs g9**, 113091607 (−$3,897): final herd and crop mix are nearly the same, and the money deficit already exists at day 12 (−$1.8k). MKGA has +32 milk and +23 fertilizer sales. It is the closest case to a same-macro efficiency problem, but only one observation and no replay-visible safe unit action identifies a causal residual.
