/workspace/shared/research/bcppo_v3_design_codex.md:61:**As data augmentation / a candidate initialisation: yes, conditionally.  As an assumed strong GPU league opponent: no, not until rollout-gated.**  `es3_g5` is a structured tape/replay controller plus repair layers.  On expert trajectories BC can learn its local actions, but a single unit/board action error changes positions and inventory; then the clone visits states absent from the teacher distribution.  The present BC state is also not a full controller state: `gpufeat.py:23-34` retains only whether YARN was among the first two shops, not the full route/tape history.  If es3 decisions distinguish histories collapsed by these features, supervised labels are irreducibly multimodal and high teacher-forced accuracy will overstate closed-loop competence.
/workspace/shared/research/bcppo_v3_design_codex.md:66:2. Run DAgger: roll out the clone, query es3_g5 on the clone's visited state, append labels.  This directly attacks covariate shift and is much higher value than merely generating more on-policy teacher games.
/workspace/shared/research/bcppo_v3_design_codex.md:80:- Retain a nonzero BC floor, e.g. `kl_floor=.01–.02` after calibrating the three-head KL scale.  The observed `kl_bc=.28–.44` says the existing `.05` coefficient does not translate to a small policy displacement, so tune from measured action KL rather than coefficient intuition.
/workspace/shared/research/bcppo_v3_design_codex.md:82:For **B=4096**, first increase `mb` to 131072 and/or train in 64–128-step rollout fragments if memory/replay time becomes the bottleneck.  Use B=4096 only if per-GPU throughput keeps improving; otherwise 2048 has better iteration latency and quicker diagnostic feedback.  Four independent single-GPU PPOs are useful for ablations, but they do not create a 4-GPU learner; for one policy, use DDP/all-reduced gradients with fixed per-rank batches and prove resume determinism.
